> \\ fcp@nuance.com (Craig Presson)   CPresson@aol.com\

Au contraire: I see deity (and hence the Deity) as precisely the kind of
unprovable assertion that E-prime seeks to cure.  Empirical question:
Do any E-prime supporters believe in God?

Philological footnote:  From what I've heard, the original Hebrew in the
Book of Exodus has more the sense "I am that is", or, less elegantly,
"I am what exists".  That the author(s) managed to write such a 
sentence in Hebrew, a language without a copula ("be" of equation), 
shows that one can still make unprovable assertions without "be".  

So, in some perverse sense, our ability to speak in E-prime requires that
E have a "be" verb.  In other words, in order for E-prime to be useful,
it must constitute a *proper* subset of E.

