Date: Thu, 3 Nov 1994 15:34:28 -0500
Subject: [#94-11-68] Neural nets - a simplified description ?

>From: Luciana Haill <lmhaill@gwent.ac.uk>
>X-Message-Number: #94-11-60
>
>...
>Neural Nets, as a department of A.I. or is it a seperate topic, main
>views and current critiques
>...
>

I'll comment on just this one.  Neural Nets is a term describing a broad
range of research some of which might be considered part of AI, some as
directly opposed to (traditional) AI, and some completely unrelated.  This
is due to the range of goals that motivate the work of people who would
call themselves Neural Net researchers.  But lumping it all together under
any single description is missing the diversity.

On one hand, there are people who are interested in engineering applications
- in some areas where traditional AI has been disappointing they're
turning to neural net approaches (the difference between traditional
AI vs. neural nets could be summarized as "sophisticated symbol manipulator"
vs. "lots of simple computational elements").  You can view these people as
AI-types since they're working toward goals similar to those of more
traditional AI researchers.  And because their goals are similar so are the
criteria by which they guide and judge their work: the bottom line is the
computational power of their creations.  You can also view these people as
opposed to the traditional AI perspective because the methods they employ and
the systems they build are usually very different from traditional AI (as are
the philosophical justifications pinned on the work...)  We can call this
major subdivision the "neural network people".  This side of neural nets
has to its credit several interesting and powerful computational algorithms.

On another hand, there are people who are interested in modeling biological
systems - again in areas where traditional cognitivist theory has been
disappointing many are turning to network theories.  These people are more
interested in explaining biological and psychological phenomena than in the
computational prowess of their models.  Thus the criteria for guiding and
judging work is different: the bottom line is how well you account for
known data and what predictions you make.  We can call this subdivision the
"neural modelers".  This side of neural nets has generated some innovative
theories of brain function.

Often, even these broad distinctions between researchers are blurred in the
descriptions presented outside the field.  So many people hearing of
neural nets might think backprop (a computationally-driven system) is
being claimed as a model of the brain.  It's particularly unfortunate when
(and this happens a lot!) a researcher in NNs does this.  When faced with
criticisms from the computational community, a neural network researcher
(for example, somebody working with backprop) often turns to the brain for
support of his/her theory.  The evidence they present is, however, often
mere hand-waving.  On the other hand, a neural modeler (for example, someone
working with Adaptive Resonance Theory) might try to point out the
computational power (in the case of ART, statistical clustering) of his/her
model.  This computational power is, however, usually not impressive to the
computational people.  In either case you present to critics in one field
evidence supporting your theory which comes from a field they're not familiar
with, and with good rhetoric you can usually get by.

Then on top of this, there's lots of hype.  The field of neural networks
(both the computational and the modeling aspects) is very suggestive of
colorful analogies and grand promises.  These are usually overblown, but
get plenty of attention.  Then, when they're finally debunked there's a
rebound effect where a lot of babies are thrown out with the bathwater.
This has already happened once with Minsky and Papert's accurate critique
_Perceptrons_ and is on the horizon for backprop (the more recent champion)
as well (problems with scaling the system on the engineering hand, and dubious
"biological plausibility" on the other).

So the bottom line I'd line to make in this lengthy comment is that
Neural Nets is a very diverse field which is often inappropriately lumped
together.  If there's any one view it's that simple elements operating in
parallel can exhibit interesting global processing.  But this does not
imply any particular network system as being representative of the field.

As for me, I'd call myself a "neural modeler", but would rather not be
associated with Neural Nets at all because I don't wish to be tossed on
those waves of fickle popularity.

Paul


