From: dkrieger@netcom.com (Dave Krieger)
Date: Thu, 3 Nov 1994 13:27:42 -0800
Subject: [#94-11-69] CORVUS CORDON BLEU:  Netscape found to be not exceptional

I forgot a maxim that I've repeated often enough to plenty of people:
"Never attribute to conspiracy what can be equally well ascribed to
stupidity."

It only took about twenty-five minutes for me to get the answers to the
questions I wanted:  Netscape (by Mosaic Communications) is not exceptional
in not letting you selectively load inline images; ONLY MacWeb lets you do
this.

One correspondent who informed me of this drew the conclusion that "They're
ALL in it TOGETHER."  I'm unwilling to go that far.

So I retract any imputation of connivance on the part of HotWired and
Mosaic Communications.  As I promised in the first post:

>If I'm wrong, and the parties involved choose to prove it in one of the
>ways I describe, I'll happily recant my suspicions in whatever forum they
>appear.

Please resend this message anywhere you saw my earlier message.

But, doggone it, doesn't anybody but MacWeb care about saving bandwidth?

Overall, the correspondence I received was polite, well-informed, and
cordial.  "Dear Sir -- You are an uniformed ass."  I have to admire anyone
polite enough to include the "Dear Sir" when calling me an ass (though I
have never worn a uniform in my life).  I will admit to having been
_uninformed_, and an ass, and I wish all my correspondents well.

I was disappointed by the number of people who replied with "Attaboy; you
tell 'em; those damn advertisers should get lost," which wasn't my point at
all.

An interesting and unexpected fact arose along the way:  if you access
HotWired via lynx, you can't see the advertising even if you want to.  This
doesn't seem fair to the advertisers.

By the way, everything I said about the economics of the Internet still
stands.  If net providers are required to screen content, the diversity of
viewpoints represented on the Net will disappear, and _only_ content backed
by large budgets will reach any audience at all.  Again:  I am in favor of
advertising; I am opposed to censorship and regulation.
